From 763e71000e66c86de3c33c57ed83eb6456392b24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:04:13 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 28/33] super-amd: add disks, delete and update volumes

- --add to the container: a new disk becomes an AMD global hot spare,
  as the BIOS menu initializes and assigns one; a former member failed
  on errors is given back and rebuilt in its slot.  A disk with a
  partition table, a Legacy (non-RAID) disk, one the configuration has
  as a non-RAID disk, or a 64th disk (the packet has room for 64 device
  records, the controller's among them) is refused; one with a file
  system or volume mdadm knows (ext2/3/4, reiserfs, XFS, btrfs, LUKS,
  LVM2) or the volumes of another AMD configuration on it is taken only
  with --force, as its ends are zeroed.  mdmon records it, so it must
  run for the container: it does while a RAID1, RAID10, RAID5 or
  RAIDABLE volume runs (not for RAID0 and Volume).  A disk with a lower
  DeviceID than the others changes the container UUID: mdadm says so
  and updates the map.
- --kill-subarray: the volume is deleted as the AMD driver deletes one
  (its transform log cleared, its LD records gone, the data left on the
  disks); not while a later volume runs, which would be renumbered.
- --update-subarray: the volume's name (not that of another volume),
  and the AMD driver's cache policy (read-cache, write-back and their
  no- forms).
- Without mdmon both write the configuration themselves, so that a write
  that fails is reported.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
